<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Principality of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1362–1372</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Principality of Aquitaine was the territory in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> granted to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Edward the Black Prince</a> in 1362, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> had freed the English lands there from French overlordship. Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, it covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, Poitou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, the Limousin and other regions. Revolts against the prince's hearth tax and the French reconquest ended it in 1372.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principality-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Principality of Aquitaine" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patricia Fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Aquitaine.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
